inline_comments.comment:
The `setTimeout` fires once; if the image is not yet `complete` at that moment it never calls `setupImage`, so the Promise will never resolve (it's neither resolved nor rejected). The load/error listeners cover images that complete after they're registered, but an image that is in-flight when the listeners are added and completes *before* the listeners fire will be missed if `img.complete` happens to be false at `setTimeout` time (extremely unlikely but theoretically possible on slow JS engines). The original code had the same pattern, but since this is being extracted into a shared utility it's worth documenting the assumption, or using a more robust guard:
```ts
setTimeout(() => {
  if (img.complete) {
    setupImage();
  } else if (img.naturalWidth > 0) {
    // already decoded, missed the event
    setupImage();
  }
});
```

`await configureLabelImages(div.node(), node.label)` — `div.node()` returns `Element | null` (the D3 contract), while `configureLabelImages` is typed as `container: HTMLElement`. If the selection were somehow empty, passing `null` here would crash at `container.getElementsByTagName('img')`. A null guard before the call would close the type gap:
```js
const divNode = div.node();
if (divNode) { await configureLabelImages(divNode, node.label); }
```
The same pattern applies in `dagre-wrapper/createLabel.js`.

`getClusterTitleWidth` is a synchronous function, but `createLabel` is `async`. Without `await`, `label` is a `Promise` object, not a DOM element, so `label.getBBox()` will throw `TypeError: label.getBBox is not a function` at runtime. The function must be made `async` and `createLabel` must be awaited:
```js
export const getClusterTitleWidth = async (elem, node) => {
  const label = await createLabel(node.label, node.labelStyle, undefined, true, false, node.width);
  ...
};
```
(The pre-existing TODO comment on the file's current `main` already acknowledges this; the PR adds parameters but doesn't fix the fundamental issue.)

general_comments.comment:
The new `addHtmlLabel` in both `createLabel.js` files appends a hidden SVG to `document.body` for measurement and removes it afterward with `tempSvg.remove()`. If an exception is thrown between the append and the remove (e.g., inside `configureLabelImages`), the temp SVG will leak into the DOM. Although `configureLabelImages` always resolves rather than rejects, a `try/finally` block would make the cleanup unconditional and resilient to future changes:
```js
try {
  tempSvg.node().appendChild(fo.node());
  await configureLabelImages(...);
  ...
} finally {
  tempSvg.remove();
}
```
